Per-site "reset on exit": clear a site's cookies and storage as soon as
its last tab closes, instead of waiting for the browser to quit.

The exclusion list added by aerium-clear-on-exit-site-exclusions.patch
answers "keep these when I quit". This answers the opposite question -
"never let this one persist at all" - for the account you do not want
following you around but do not want to give up every other login to
avoid. Marking a site here is independent of the delete-on-exit toggle:
it works whether or not that toggle is on, because it is about one site
rather than about shutdown.

Scope is deliberately cookies and storage only. Nothing else can be
honestly offered: ChromeBrowsingDataRemoverDelegate::RemoveEmbedderData()
CHECKs - not DCHECKs, so it aborts release builds too - that a filtered
removal's mask is a subset of FILTERABLE_DATA_TYPES, and history,
autofill, passwords and site settings are all outside it because their
backends ignore the origin filter entirely. A UI implying a full per-site
wipe would be a lie, so the string says what it does.

Implemented on ChromeBrowsingDataLifetimeManager rather than as a new
KeyedService. That class is already the per-profile owner of on-exit
clearing, already holds the BrowsingDataRemover plumbing, and - most
usefully - already enumerates every open tab for a profile in
GetOpenedUrlsAndOngoingDownloads(), including the Android variant. Keeping
both behaviours in one place also stops the on-exit and on-tab-close paths
drifting apart as the filtering rules change. A separate service would
have meant a new factory, an entry in profile_keyed_service_factories.cc
and a hunk in chrome/browser/BUILD.gn, for no behavioural gain.

Entries are folded to their registrable domain (eTLD+1) exactly as the
exclusion list folds its own, so marking example.com covers
www.example.com too and the two lists cannot disagree about what a "site"
is.

